★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**Retry Count Strategy by Bottleneck Type:**
The retry counts reflect the underlying concurrency patterns: (1) **Printer sync uses 10** because optimistic locking creates exponential retry pressure when multiple batches collide—each retry must re-read and re-compare the version, making exponential backoff essential to avoid live-lock. (2) **Android sync uses 3** because transaction timeouts are generous (15s) and semaphore + delay already spread out retries naturally. (3) **Batch/UI services use 3** because they fail-fast to propagate errors upward rather than retry indefinitely. (4) **Tests use 20-30** to tolerate infrastructure slowness (Docker startup, CI resource constraints) without flaking. This pattern shows a key architectural trade-off: high-concurrency systems (printer sync) need more retries with backoff, while sequential systems (Android sync) rely on timeouts and can use fewer retries.
